{
  "name": "A Blueprint for African Poultry Success",
  "parent_id": 203314067212801,
  "group_id": "dfa411f7-6a8f-4f59-a6a2-9a1ae430935c",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:20.455Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:20.536Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:20.523Z",
  "id": 203989731179800,
  "uuid": "0d70573e-ae4e-4b63-8bfa-4cb534fa525a",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "e018f51a-4410-a4db-ba61-ec31a39180ce",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "978dc12b-c4fc-cf0b-14cb-1ce497ae07b2",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Organized by Community Engagement Training Officer Sosthenes S. Milobo, the event brought together participants from twelve villages, including Ilkiushin, Engutoto, and Losikito, to learn critical strategies from Mr. Jonas Tungi, a poultry specialist from Kibo Poultry Company.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The training emphasized that success begins with meticulous planning. Farmers learned the importance of proper house allocation (the chicks' nursery) and selecting quality, single-parent line chicks. For broilers, the initial weight is a key performance indicator: healthy chicks should weigh 38-40 grams at hatching and reach about 200 grams by day seven.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "For the first 12 days, chicks must be kept in a brooder where warmth and hygiene are non-negotiable. Mr. Tungi advised closely monitoring the ambient temperature, ideally between 24-32 degrees Celsius, cautioning against overcrowding. A simple visual check - chicks spreading out - indicates the right temperature, though a thermometer is the best tool for accuracy. A quality poultry house must ensure fresh air circulation, pest exclusion, and adequate space, starting at 10 chicks per square meter, increasing as the birds grow.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Nutrition is pivotal. Farmers were taught the correct sequence: glucose water for the first two hours, followed by clean water and starter feed. A critical warning was issued against using contaminated feed, especially maize that may contain Aflatoxin, which poses risks to both birds and consumers. Farmers who mix their own feed must incorporate a toxin binder. Broiler chickens, which are ready for market in five to seven weeks, require four bags of feed per 100 birds (one starter, three grower), while layers demand a different schedule and up to two years of care.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "To combat common threats like Newcastle, Gumboro, and fowl pox, strict biosecurity is essential, meaning limiting visitors and maintaining high hygiene standards. Crucially, even indigenous chickens must be vaccinated on schedule, with vitamin supplements administered afterward to aid recovery.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The session also addressed financial sustainability. Farmers were strongly encouraged to form cooperative groups to ease access to loans and improve market power. Unity makes it easier to share market information, negotiate better prices, and tap into growing online demand, particularly on social media platforms like WhatsApp.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Poultry farming is fundamentally a science and a business. With strategic planning, quality inputs, and disciplined disease control, the Tanzanian farmers trained in Arusha are now equipped to transform their poultry flocks into a reliable and sustainable source of income and nutrition for their families and communities.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
